(I'll put useful things I learnt down here, if I find it oppurtune)

* Internet is a way to connect computers all together and ensure that, whatever happens, they find a way to stay connected
* the internet is a wire of different phisical form which connects devices
* a server is a special computer connected directly to the internet and web pages are files on that servers hard drive
* every server has an unique Internet Protocol address (IP address), such as google's 8.8.8.8, which ofter has a more human-readable name related to it, dalled "domain name"

Our own computer are not connected directly to the internet though, and are called "clients".
* clients are connected to the internet trough an ISP (Internet Service Provider)
* connections transport informations broken in packets, smaller chunks of it (like a puzzle piece)
* everything connected to the internet directly (such as a server) or indirectly (such as a client) have an IP address
* everytime two connections intersect there's a piece of equipment called "router" (like the one we have at home, provided by our ISP)
* routers direct packages around the internet helping each packet to get closer to its destination

Each packet is like a piece of candy, wrapped in layers of informations:
* the first layers is the client device private IP address
* the client (our laptop) sends the packet to the router which adds its own IP address
* each time the packet reaches a new router, another layer is added until it reaches the server
* the server may andwer with another packet containing the exact same wrappings, which get unfolder by each router to send it back to the client
(credits to Aaron Titus and Karissa Phelps for the useful video in the MDN guide https://youtu.be/7_LPdttKXPc?si=emsWYq32_58l6k4B)

We said that each server has an IP address and a more human-understandable name, the domain name.
* a DNS (Domain name Server) provides a Domain Name Service which translates, like a disctionary, each domain name into its respective IP address
Basically the path is:
* you open the browser and type a domain name (eg: ilikepotatoes.com)
* the browser sends a request to the DNS server to get the corresponding IP address
* after getting the IP address the browser forwards the request to the data center
* datacenter sends information back and the packet goes until it reaches the home/office router
* this router send the packet to the client device

Btw it's not necessary that all packet are routed troug hthe same path: each packet can travel indipendently depending on the best route avayable at that time
* there is a process called aknowledgment to verify all packets have reached destination
* in case a packet gets lost it can be sent again by thge sender
Real-life communication is a lot crowded and must be efficient.
* protocols are different standard used to manage packets/data flow and their travel 
* protocols set rules for data packet conversion, attachments and source/destination of the packet

